<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bohdan Khmelnytsky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1595–<a href="/years/1657">1657</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bohdan Khmelnytsky was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> leader who led a major uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> from 1648. The revolt caused catastrophic losses and led to an alliance with Muscovy. His forces were among the invaders during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-deluge">Deluge</a> of 1655–1660, which devastated the Commonwealth and accelerated its decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky.jpg" alt="Bohdan Khmelnytsky" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bohdan_Khmelnytsky.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zynoviy Bohdan Mykhailovych Khmelnytsky was a Ruthenian nobleman of the Abdank coat of arms. His date of birth is not firmly known. He was baptised Theodore, in Ukrainian Bohdan, and used Zynoviy on religious occasions but signed as Bohdan in daily life. After his studies he entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> service in 1617. His father was killed at the Battle of Cecora on 17 September 1620, and Khmelnytsky was captured by the Turks and spent two years in captivity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. In 1637, as military scribe of the Zaporozhian Host, he signed the rebels' capitulation at Borowica after a Cossack revolt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the Czapliński Affair the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chyhyryn">Chyhyryn</a> starosta Daniel Czapliński was authorised to seize Khmelnytsky's Subotiv estate. This is generally regarded as the immediate cause of the uprising, though it was chiefly a catalyst for wider discontent among Orthodox Ruthenians over religion, ethnicity and economics. At the end of January 1648 a Cossack Rada unanimously elected him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetman</a>, and he sought an alliance with the Khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, İslâm III Giray. In 1648–1649 the Cossacks under his leadership massacred tens of thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, and others were handed over as slaves to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zboriv">Treaty of Zboriv</a> of 18 August 1649 was unfavourable to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>. At Berestechko on 18 June 1651 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> betrayed him and held him captive, and the Cossacks suffered about 30,000 casualties. On 18 January 1654 the Cossack Rada concluded the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereiaslav</a>, accepting the overlordship of Tsar Alexei Mikhailovich. Historians disagree whether he intended a military union, a suzerainty or full incorporation into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Khmelnytsky died on 6 August 1657. His rising permanently changed the balance of power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> exploited the weakening of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Opinions of him differ sharply. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> he is generally regarded as a national hero, and a city and a region bear his name. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> called him the Scourge of God and blamed him for the decline of the Commonwealth. Jewish historiography judges him overwhelmingly negatively, since the uprising killed an estimated 18,000 to 100,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. Russian imperial and Soviet historiography presented him as the unifier of Ukraine with Russia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bohdan Khmelnytsky” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After victories over the Poles, <b>Bohdan Khmelnytsky</b> entered Kyiv at Christmas 1648, hailed as a liberator from Polish captivity, and in February 1649 told a Polish delegation at Pereiaslav that he meant to be hetman of a Ruthenia stretching to Chelm and Halych.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The khans pursued their own policy in Eastern Europe: Islam III Giray allied with the Cossack hetman <b>Bohdan Khmelnytsky</b> in 1648, contributing to victories at Zhovti Vody and Korsun before abandoning him at Berestechko in 1651, and Mehmed IV Giray helped Hetman Ivan Vyhovsky defeat a Russian army…</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Khmelnytsky Uprising, the Cossack revolt led by <b>Bohdan Khmelnytsky</b> from 1648, inflicted catastrophic losses on Ukraine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Khmelnytsky Uprising against the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth, after his victories over the Poles, <b>Bohdan Khmelnytsky</b> entered Kyiv at Christmas 1648, hailed as a liberator from Polish captivity.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>Hetman <b>Bohdan Khmelnytsky</b> and his Cossack officers swore allegiance to Tsar Alexei in return for protection against Poland-Lithuania.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-18">18 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bohdan Khmelnytsky's</b> uprising began there in 1648.</blockquote><a href="/state/zaporozhian-sich">Zaporozhian Sich</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Subotiv, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chyhyryn">Chyhyryn</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.1,32.56&amp;year=1635" aria-label="Subotiv on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Bohdan Khmelnytsky"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5143 5924h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5143 5924h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.1,32.56&amp;year=1635">What was here in 1635? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
